course: dictionary
lesson: 15232
title: Shan't, and Shan
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shan is not a word you can use on its own. It is a piece of the word «shan't», which is a short form of «shall not». Shan't is squeezed together from shall and not, so the apostrophe shows where letters are missing.
explain: «I shan't go to the party.» means «I shall not go to the party.» Shan't is mostly British and a bit old-fashioned. Most people today say «won't» instead, as in «I won't go».
explain: Alice in Wonderland uses shan't. As she grows tall, she cries, «I shan't be able!» Shan is also a name, with a capital letter: the Shan are a people of Myanmar (Burma) and nearby lands. Shan State is in Myanmar.
fact: Shan is a fragment: a piece of the word «shan't».
fact: «Shan't» means «shall not».
fact: «Shan't» is mostly British and old-fashioned, and most people today say «won't».
fact: The Shan are a people of Myanmar (Burma) and nearby lands, with their own language.
fact: Shan State is a region in Myanmar.
exercise: «I shan't be late.» What does shan't mean? | shall | shun | *shall not | should
exercise: Which word do most people use today instead of «shan't»? | shall | should | shan | *won't
exercise: Shan State is in which country? | Jamaica | Scotland | Australia | *Myanmar
test: Can shan be used alone as an ordinary word in English? | Yes, it means shall | *No, it is only part of shan't | Yes, in Scotland
test: Which people, with their own language, are linked to the name Shan? | Aussies | *The Shan of Myanmar and nearby lands | Scots | Maori
exam: Alice says «I shan't be able!» Which word would a modern speaker most likely use instead? | Shall | *Won't | Shan | Should
